// Sample family: every person and record cited here is fictional.
// Living people: birth years and public family relationships only.

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Jory, Tamsin and Nora Penhallow are the children of #refp("david-penhallow") and #refp("karin-solberg") of Madison. David, the son of Robert and Joan Penhallow, was born in Madison in 1958.#src("david-birth") Karin, the daughter of Arne and Ruth Solberg, was born there in 1961.#src("karin-birth") They married in Madison in 1993.#src("david-karin-marriage")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Their son Jory was born in 1996, and their daughter Tamsin in 1999.#src("jory-penhallow::jory-birth")#src("tamsin-birth") Nora was born in 2003 and joined the family by adoption the next year.#src("nora-penhallow::nora-birth")#src("nora-penhallow::nora-adoption") She is the youngest of the three.
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Tamsin married Erik Lund in Madison in 2025.#src("tamsin-erik-marriage") Erik was born in Minneapolis in 1997.#src("erik-lund::erik-birth") His own family is not part of this book.
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
On their father's side, Jory, Tamsin and Nora are the fifth generation of the family in Wisconsin. Their great-great-grandparents, John and Elizabeth Penhallow, arrived from Cornwall in 1868 and settled at Mineral Point.#src("john-penhallow::john-arrival-1868") On their mother's side, the family came to Madison when their grandfather Arne Solberg moved there from Minneapolis to study engineering.#src("arne-solberg::arne-engineer")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Tamsin gathered the records behind this history. In 2023 she recorded her grandmother Ruth's memories of the Solberg and Kessler families, which are the main source for the second chapter.#src("tamsin-interview-2023")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Three family papers connect the generations. The first is the Bible register that Edith Penhallow began when she married in 1907.#src("edith-may-rowe::penhallow-rowe-marriage-bible") The second is the letter she wrote at Easter 1944 to her son Robert, who was serving overseas.#src("edith-may-rowe::edith-letter-1944") The third is the recipe booklet Joan Penhallow typed for Christmas 1995, with dishes from both sides of the family.#src("joan-treloar::joan-recipe-booklet-1995")
]

On their father's side, the first chapter followed the families of #refp("robert-john-penhallow") and #refp("joan-treloar"), back to a tin miner's baptism at St Just. On their mother's side, the second chapter followed the families of #refp("arne-solberg") and #refp("ruth-kessler") in Minneapolis and Madison.
